 washington's mayor has  asked the federal government to deploy  the national guard indefinitely i've  asked for the deployment of the guard as  long as we need the guard to deal with  the humanitarian crisis  that we expect to escalate the city is  caught in the middle of a feud between  texas governor greg abbott and the biden  administration abbott vowed to bust  undocumented migrants to d.c if  president joe biden didn't stop them  from crossing into his state we are  sending them to the united states  capital where the body administration  will be able to more immediately address  the needs of the people that they are  allowing to come across our border the  migrants say when they arrived in texas  they were given the option to travel to  washington under false promises never  imagining they'd be left without food or  shelter  they told us with lies they told us to  come to d.c they have help here they  have work here they offer you  they offer you jobs  tell you that they're going to help you  but then they just drop you off here  like an animal many of the migrants are  now sleeping outdoors in the summer heat  on the grounds near the u.s capitol  local charities are trying to help but  they can't keep up it's really difficult  the funds have run out so we can't send  people right away to their final  destination we have to remember the  majority of people do not want to stay  charities are begging the d.c government  for money to provide basic necessities  or send the migrants to other cities  where they have family or friends we  have never received any money from the  d.c government or any government all our  money has been grassroots raised the  federal emergency management agency has  given the city over 2 million dollars in  aid but dc's mayor is asking the white  house for millions more we know that the  federal government does this because we  see this type of response  with afghan refugees  and now we see it with people who are  coming to united states from ukraine  in the meantime migrants like anna  karina are stranded their dreams of a  better life in america lay shattered at  the foot of the nation's capital  jennifer johnson global news washington  the first january 6 defendant to go to  trial has been sentenced to more than  seven years in prison prosecutors say  guy refit led the mob charging the  police line outside the west steps of  the u.s capitol while carrying a gun and  zip ties at the time refit was a member  of the texas three percenters a  far-right militia but now says he no  longer wants to have any association  with the group  inspiration against addiction next the  artist drawing hope at the epicenter of  bc's drug crisis  today black communities across canada  are reflecting on their heritage and  continuing calls for change on this  emancipation day  [Music]  celebrations of black history mixed with  demands for more systemic action against  racism in events like these in halifax  as black canadians marked 188 years  since britain abolished slavery across  the colonies that saw more than eight  000 people of african descent freed in  1834  but not before millions were enslaved  throughout the british empire's history  as an epicenter of the toxic drug crisis  homelessness and crime vancouver's  downtown east side has a reputation as  one of the toughest and most challenged  neighborhoods in the entire country but  one vancouver artist says the community  has been both his inspiration and  salvation and he credits it for helping  him overcome addiction and depression  kristin robinson has the story  being able to set up for a public art  show every saturday has been a long  journey for chris blake cook i never  thought i would survive myself long  enough to see any of my dreams come true  how much is that one i have a list  few buyers are aware the young artist  emerged from darkness  while battling depression in alberta at  the start of covid cook turned to hard  drugs and alcohol he says